+**Gates.** A case is qualified through six quality-control gates, whose verdicts are recorded in its family's QC
+profile. Gate 0 requires that the profile exists; Gate 1, that the worlds are valid, distinct, split and leave headroom;
+Gate 2, that actions change the intended state and that every score is rebuilt from sealed evidence; Gate 3, that a
+better-informed policy does better and that shortcuts lose; Gate 4, that the result supports the attribution it claims;
+and Gate 5, that the comparison was sized, frozen and executed without tuning on outcomes. Each gate has one of five
+statuses (`passed`, `failed`, `partial`, `not_run` or `not_applicable`), and only `passed` qualifies a result. We wrote
+the profiles after the initial runs. The confirmatory run of the agreement case came after its profile. Housing and
+Procurement pass four of Gates 1 to 5, all except Gate 4, and no case yet passes all five (Table 6.1).
 
-**The reference is played on every world.** For each case a test plays the reference through the
-real environment and verifier on every admitted world, with no model, and checks that it reaches
-its declared result: the Housing reference and a full-information tenant reproduce the published
-values in all 600 v14 cells; Procurement's best policy equals the environment's average over its
-27 type profiles in all 24 worlds; the data-center oracle pair loses nothing in all 116 worlds of
-both development packs and all 60 of the admitted confirmatory pack; Refund V2.5's release requires full credit on every case. The initial tests followed development; the admitted-pack dry run preceded the confirmatory; two of them (the data-center grade and the Housing ceiling) are shown to fail when the grade
-or the reference is wrong.
